<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyiv</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kiev</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kyiv is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> river. From the late 9th century it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, the first East Slavic state, whose prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a> accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 988. Sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1240, it later came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and became the capital of independent Ukraine in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyiv.jpg" alt="Kyiv" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Anzola · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kyiv_(234807751).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kyiv lies on both banks of the Dnieper River, which flows southward through the city toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. The older and higher western part sits on wooded hills with ravines and small rivers, and these gave the city toponyms such as Podil (lower), Pechersk (caves) and uzviz (a steep street). The city expanded onto the low left bank to the east only in the 20th century. Its name is traditionally derived from Kyi, one of four founders named in tradition, though the linguist Jaroslav Rudnyckyj reads it as meaning palisaded settlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first people in the region lived there in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a>. Roman coins of the 2nd to 4th centuries suggest trade with the eastern provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. Scholars debate when the city was founded. The traditional date is 482 CE, so the city marked its 1,500th anniversary in 1982, while archaeology points to the sixth or seventh century, and some researchers date it as late as the late 9th century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyi-shchek-and-khoryv">Kyi, Shchek and Khoryv</a> and their sister Lybid are the founders.</p><p>A Slavic settlement on the trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, it was a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> until its capture by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> in the mid-9th century. Under Varangian rule it became a capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, the first East Slavic state. The Siege of Kiev in 1240 left it in ruins, and it lost most of its influence for centuries. It grew into a centre of Eastern Orthodox learning in the 16th century, and the Mohyla Academy was founded as a theological school in 1632.</p><p>The city prospered during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in the late 19th century. In 1918 the Ukrainian People's Republic declared independence and made Kyiv its capital. After the Ukrainian-Soviet and Polish-Soviet wars it became part of the Ukrainian SSR, whose capital it became in 1934. It suffered significant destruction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> and recovered as the third-largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. It became the capital of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kyiv is the capital and most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, with 2,952,301 inhabitants in January 2022. It has a special legal status as a city that is also treated as a region of the country. It remains a centre of Eastern Orthodox Christianity, and the Saint Sophia Cathedral and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-pechersk-lavra">Kyiv Pechersk Lavra</a>, the Monastery of the Caves, form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Taras Shevchenko University, founded in 1834, is the oldest university in continuous operation there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kyiv” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After victories over the Poles, Bohdan Khmelnytsky entered <b>Kyiv</b> at Christmas 1648, hailed as a liberator from Polish captivity, and in February 1649 told a Polish delegation at Pereiaslav that he meant to be hetman of a Ruthenia stretching to Chelm and Halych.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ryazan fell in December 1237, Vladimir in February 1238 and <b>Kiev</b> in December 1240.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same time it expanded eastwards: Algirdas defeated the Golden Horde at the Battle of Blue Waters around 1362, opening the way to <b>Kiev</b> and Podolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Primary Chronicle, compiled in <b>Kiev</b> in the early twelfth century, the Slavic and Finnic tribes of the north invited the Varangian Rurik to rule them in 862, and in 882 his kinsman Oleg came south from Novgorod, killed the local rulers Askold and Dir and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As the second city of Kievan Rus’, Novgorod was usually governed by a son of the grand prince of <b>Kiev</b>: Vladimir the Great and Yaroslav the Wise both ruled there before taking the throne in Kiev, and the stone Cathedral of Saint Sophia was built in 1045–1050.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>This gave the Polish nobility the leverage to insist on terms favourable to the Crown, including the transfer of Podlachia, Volhynia and the large Ukrainian palatinates of <b>Kiev</b> and Bracław from Lithuanian to Polish administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
